WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.072
Hearing each string.

2
00:00:01.372 --> 00:00:03.375
Part two of Continuo: how it works.

3
00:00:03.676 --> 00:00:05.779
How the band gets to hear every string on

4
00:00:05.779 --> 00:00:06.180
its own.

5
00:00:08.232 --> 00:00:10.570
A guitar has six strings, and a microphone

6
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:11.765
hears them all at once.

7
00:00:12.076 --> 00:00:14.258
Every note from every string lands in the

8
00:00:14.258 --> 00:00:15.193
same mixed signal.

9
00:00:15.505 --> 00:00:17.635
This video is about the first two stages

10
00:00:17.635 --> 00:00:19.973
after the guitar: the pickup that hears

11
00:00:19.973 --> 00:00:22.311
each string on its own, and the board that

12
00:00:22.311 --> 00:00:24.285
turns six strings into six streams of

13
00:00:24.285 --> 00:00:24.700
numbers.

14
00:00:28.327 --> 00:00:29.283
Here is the catch.

15
00:00:29.602 --> 00:00:31.886
Two strings can sound almost the same note

16
00:00:31.886 --> 00:00:32.790
at the same time.

17
00:00:33.108 --> 00:00:34.968
A microphone hears the sum of both.

18
00:00:35.463 --> 00:00:38.084
On its own, each string is a clean, narrow

19
00:00:38.084 --> 00:00:39.088
peak at its pitch.

20
00:00:39.423 --> 00:00:41.988
On a guitar this happens often: a string

21
00:00:41.988 --> 00:00:44.107
sounds its note and fainter overtones

22
00:00:44.107 --> 00:00:46.449
above it, and one string's overtone can

23
00:00:46.449 --> 00:00:48.680
land almost exactly on another string's

24
00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:48.959
note.

25
00:00:49.837 --> 00:00:51.920
Mixed together, the two peaks blur into

26
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:52.119
one.

27
00:00:52.416 --> 00:00:54.549
The microphone cannot tell that there were

28
00:00:54.549 --> 00:00:56.633
two notes, let alone which strings they

29
00:00:56.633 --> 00:00:57.129
came from.

30
00:00:58.072 --> 00:00:59.724
So we did not solve that problem.

31
00:01:00.025 --> 00:01:00.726
We removed it.

32
00:01:01.026 --> 00:01:03.079
If every string had its own ear, there

33
00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:04.532
would be nothing to untangle.

34
00:01:08.707 --> 00:01:11.493
The pickup is a Roland GK-3, a hexaphonic

35
00:01:11.493 --> 00:01:11.937
pickup.

36
00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:15.167
It has six small magnetic coils, one under

37
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:15.927
each string.

38
00:01:16.452 --> 00:01:18.612
Pluck one string, and mostly its coil

39
00:01:18.612 --> 00:01:20.718
answers: one string, one signal.

40
00:01:21.042 --> 00:01:23.148
The other five lanes stay almost quiet.

41
00:01:23.877 --> 00:01:26.484
Play a chord, all six strings at once, and

42
00:01:26.484 --> 00:01:28.757
the pickup still hands over six separate

43
00:01:28.757 --> 00:01:30.199
signals, one per string.

44
00:01:30.532 --> 00:01:32.806
A chord becomes six single notes, kept

45
00:01:32.806 --> 00:01:34.525
apart all the way to the board.

46
00:01:35.481 --> 00:01:37.716
And the two strings from before, on nearly

47
00:01:37.716 --> 00:01:38.412
the same note?

48
00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:40.647
They now arrive on two different lanes.

49
00:01:40.945 --> 00:01:42.633
There is nothing left to untangle.

50
00:01:44.187 --> 00:01:45.272
Here is the real one.

51
00:01:45.582 --> 00:01:47.750
The thin bar under the strings, just in

52
00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:49.661
front of the bridge, is the pickup.

53
00:01:49.971 --> 00:01:52.037
Its cable runs to this board resting on

54
00:01:52.037 --> 00:01:54.464
the guitar's body: Sena's front-end board.

55
00:01:54.774 --> 00:01:56.684
The six small blue trimmers are gain

56
00:01:56.684 --> 00:01:59.111
controls, one per string, so Sena can even

57
00:01:59.111 --> 00:01:59.937
the strings out.

58
00:02:00.669 --> 00:02:02.819
Each of the pickup's six coils sits right

59
00:02:02.819 --> 00:02:05.071
under its own string, and the six signals

60
00:02:05.071 --> 00:02:07.322
leave down that one cable, still separate.

61
00:02:08.783 --> 00:02:10.791
On the guitar it looks like this: the

62
00:02:10.791 --> 00:02:12.799
board on the body, and the cable running

63
00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:14.293
to the pickup under the strings.

64
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.474
Six signals come into the board, one wire

65
00:02:22.474 --> 00:02:23.043
per string.

66
00:02:23.353 --> 00:02:25.783
Each one first gets its own gain: a quiet

67
00:02:25.783 --> 00:02:27.799
string can be turned up and a loud one

68
00:02:27.799 --> 00:02:29.918
down, so all six sit at the same level.

69
00:02:30.228 --> 00:02:31.521
Then a filter smooths it.

70
00:02:31.831 --> 00:02:34.002
Then a converter measures the smooth wave

71
00:02:34.002 --> 00:02:36.121
over and over and turns it into a stream

72
00:02:36.121 --> 00:02:36.690
of numbers.

73
00:02:37.417 --> 00:02:37.935
How often?

74
00:02:38.246 --> 00:02:40.525
Ninety-six thousand numbers a second, for

75
00:02:40.525 --> 00:02:41.198
every string.

76
00:02:41.509 --> 00:02:43.788
Each number is twenty-four bits deep, and

77
00:02:43.788 --> 00:02:45.860
all six strings are sampled at the same

78
00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:48.087
instant, so the six streams stay in step.

79
00:02:48.822 --> 00:02:50.886
Out of the board come three data wires,

80
00:02:50.886 --> 00:02:52.951
two strings on each, and a shared clock

81
00:02:52.951 --> 00:02:54.327
wire, running to the chip.

82
00:02:55.893 --> 00:02:57.888
This is the board's clock wire on an

83
00:02:57.888 --> 00:03:00.153
oscilloscope, one tick for every sample.

84
00:03:00.477 --> 00:03:02.903
The voltage is either high or low, nothing

85
00:03:02.903 --> 00:03:05.492
in between: ones and zeros, and the chip

86
00:03:05.492 --> 00:03:06.678
reads them as numbers.

87
00:03:07.002 --> 00:03:09.159
Six streams of numbers, into the chip.

88
00:03:10.803 --> 00:03:13.253
So: six strings, six coils, six streams of

89
00:03:13.253 --> 00:03:13.630
numbers.

90
00:03:13.913 --> 00:03:15.610
Next: the chip finds the notes.

